WEBVTT

00:03.960 --> 00:05.000
So what is exactly?

00:05.040 --> 00:08.360
SQL everything generate data and data is everywhere.

00:08.360 --> 00:09.640
Your first name is data.

00:09.680 --> 00:12.680
Your mobile and everything inside the mobile is data.

00:12.720 --> 00:14.840
Car is as well generating a lot of data.

00:14.880 --> 00:16.480
Bank your finance statements.

00:16.520 --> 00:17.480
Everything is data.

00:17.520 --> 00:20.080
And now of course the question is where do we store our data?

00:20.120 --> 00:25.440
Personally, we store a lot of our data in like Excel spreadsheets in a text file.

00:25.440 --> 00:27.960
So you store a lot of your data in different files.

00:28.000 --> 00:29.360
Now how about companies?

00:29.360 --> 00:34.840
They have a lot of things that generate a lot of data that the products that they produce, their customers

00:34.840 --> 00:38.840
as well, generating a lot of data and sales information and a lot of things.

00:38.840 --> 00:41.640
So companies generate massive amounts of data.

00:41.640 --> 00:45.000
So now the big question is how they handle the data, how they store it.

00:45.040 --> 00:47.680
Of course they cannot go and use like simple files.

00:47.680 --> 00:50.360
They need something bigger, stronger and smarter.

00:50.360 --> 00:52.400
And here where the database comes in.

00:52.400 --> 00:53.680
So think about the database.

00:53.680 --> 00:56.120
It's like a container for storing data.

00:56.120 --> 01:02.760
But instead of just dumping files into folders, the database organizes the data so it is easy to access

01:02.760 --> 01:04.240
to manage and to search.

01:04.240 --> 01:07.840
So a database simply it is a container that stores data.

01:07.840 --> 01:10.280
So now you might ask why we are using database.

01:10.280 --> 01:12.800
Can't we just use files like I do it personally?

01:12.800 --> 01:14.800
Well let me tell you why we use databases.

01:14.800 --> 01:17.120
Imagine that someone asks the following question.

01:17.160 --> 01:19.880
Go and find the total spending in your data.

01:19.880 --> 01:25.320
So now in order for Mike to find the total spending and the costs, he will be opening each of those

01:25.320 --> 01:29.720
files one by one, searching for the costs, trying to combine the data.

01:29.720 --> 01:32.440
And it's going to be very long and messy process.

01:32.440 --> 01:37.040
But now in the other side, if your data in database and you want to ask a question, it's going to

01:37.040 --> 01:37.920
be very easy.

01:37.920 --> 01:41.240
So all what you have to do is to talk to the database to ask a question.

01:41.240 --> 01:44.280
And the database can answer your question with a result.

01:44.320 --> 01:48.080
And now comes, of course, the question how do we talk to a database.

01:48.120 --> 01:50.320
Well we use SQL.

01:50.400 --> 01:54.840
SQL is the language that you use in order to talk to the database.

01:54.880 --> 01:58.840
It stands for Structured Query Language SQL.

01:59.240 --> 02:03.760
And here you have people that call it SQL like me and others that call it SQL.

02:03.760 --> 02:08.320
There is no right and wrong, but if you follow me through the course, I think you will start saying

02:08.490 --> 02:09.050
SQL.

02:09.090 --> 02:14.970
So by using SQL you can ask the database, you can ask your data, and the database can answer your

02:14.970 --> 02:17.050
question by sending you a result.

02:17.050 --> 02:20.010
So this process is very easy, simple and fast.

02:20.010 --> 02:23.450
And this is way better than having your data stored in different files.

02:23.490 --> 02:28.410
Another reason why we use databases is that they can handle really huge amounts of data.

02:28.410 --> 02:31.930
So sometimes we have like millions of data inside our database.

02:31.930 --> 02:36.570
But in the other side, if you are storing your data inside spreadsheets and you have like massive amount

02:36.610 --> 02:37.890
of data, what can happen?

02:37.890 --> 02:39.810
Your spreadsheets can just break.

02:39.850 --> 02:41.970
They simply can't handle big data.

02:41.970 --> 02:44.170
And another reason why we use databases.

02:44.210 --> 02:45.690
Thus it is just secure.

02:45.690 --> 02:51.850
It is safer to store important and critical data inside a database than just storing it in spreadsheets

02:51.850 --> 02:52.450
and files.

02:52.450 --> 02:56.450
So the databases are secure and you can control who is accessing what.

02:56.490 --> 03:00.330
So it is just more professional to store the data inside a database.

03:00.370 --> 03:05.370
All right my friends, so far what we have learned, most of the companies stores their data inside

03:05.370 --> 03:07.410
a container called database.

03:07.410 --> 03:12.570
And for you, in order to ask questions and to talk to your database, you have to speak the language

03:12.570 --> 03:13.650
of SQL.
